cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  TwinLight: A Multi-Timescale Optical Network Digital Twin with T-API
  Interfaces
message: >-
  If you use TwinLight in work that you publish, please cite it using the
  metadata in this file. Please also cite the underlying physical-layer
  models you rely on — GNPy and the GN/EGN model in particular; see the
  References section of the README.
type: software
# TODO(maintainer): add `affiliation:` to the author entry below — it improves
# citation indexing, and only you can supply it accurately.
authors:
  - given-names: Carlos
    family-names: Natalino
    orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7501-5547'
repository-code: 'https://github.com/carlosnatalino/TwinLight'
url: 'https://github.com/carlosnatalino/TwinLight'
abstract: >-
  TwinLight is a digital twin of an optical network that combines a
  physics-based quality-of-transmission baseline — computed either with
  GNPy split-step propagation or with a closed-form GN/EGN kernel — with
  four analytical models of time-varying impairments: EDFA gain-reservoir
  transients, polarization-mode dispersion drift and polarization-dependent
  loss, equalization-enhanced phase noise, and environmental thermal drift.
  The resulting multi-timescale signal quality is exposed through
  standards-compliant interfaces: Transport API (T-API) v2.6.0 REST, gNMI
  streaming telemetry, and Prometheus metrics. It also implements
  quality-of-transmission-aware routing, modulation and spectrum assignment
  on a flexible grid. TwinLight is intended as a realistic emulator of an
  optical domain controller, for validating software-defined networking
  control logic, running what-if and failure scenarios, generating labelled
  optical performance monitoring data for machine learning, and reproducible
  optical network planning research.
keywords:
  - optical networks
  - digital twin
  - Transport API
  - T-API
  - GNPy
  - quality of transmission
  - QoT estimation
  - optical performance monitoring
  - elastic optical networks
  - RMSA
  - gNMI
  - software-defined networking
license: MIT
version: 0.1.0
date-released: '2026-07-23'
